# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# The bridge binary: `go build ./cmd/herdr-agent` drops a 13 MB executable here,
# and the deny list above only covers Windows and shared-library suffixes.
/herdr-agent

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts
*.out
coverage.*
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# env file
.env

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
# .vscode/

# --- herdr-agent ---
# macOS
.DS_Store

# Build output
/herdr-agent
/ha

# Orchestration scaffolding: the workflow scripts used to build this, plus the
# review findings they consumed. Useful locally, noise in the history, and they
# embed absolute paths from the machine they ran on.
.workflows/

# Bridge runtime state. Never commit: it holds the chat/agent bindings and the
# pid file, and .env holds the Feishu app secret.
.herdr-agent/
*.pid

# Local herdr-agent config produced from deploy/config.example.toml — it names
# the operator's Feishu open_id and chat id.
config.toml

# Specs and acceptance scripts stay local: they quote absolute paths from the
# machine they were written on and document this operator's Feishu app setup.
# The measured facts they record (G1..G17) are what the code comments cite, so
# keep the local copy — the code alone does not explain itself.
specs/
